{"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph"},"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"@frozen","kind":"attribute"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FunctionalGraph","kind":"identifier"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Following any vertex’s edges eventually repeats, so each weakly connected component holds","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"exactly one cycle, with rooted trees hanging off its vertices (Flajolet and Odlyzko, “Random","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"mapping statistics”, EUROCRYPT ’89). ","type":"text"},{"code":"cycleDecomposition()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" finds every cycle and tree in O(n);","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"successor(of:steps:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" answers f^k(v) for any k.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"For f given as a closure over a state space too large to store, use "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"floydCycleDetection"},{"type":"text","text":" or"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"brentCycleDetection"},{"type":"text","text":", which need only "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Equatable"},{"type":"text","text":" states and O(1) memory."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The graph stores one "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":" per vertex, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"f(v)"},{"type":"text","text":". The edge leaving "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" has position "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":", so edge"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"positions and edge indices are both the source. For "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Hashable"},{"type":"text","text":" vertex labels, use"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"LabeledGraph<Label, FunctionalGraph>"},{"type":"text","text":"; "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"LabeledGraph(FunctionalGraph(g)!, labels: g.vertices)"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"gives a graph "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"g"},{"type":"text","text":" its labels back."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The graph is immutable: build a new one to change f.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs"]]},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The graph of a function f on the vertices "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<n"},{"type":"text","text":": every vertex "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"type":"text","text":" has exactly one out-edge,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v → f(v)"},{"type":"text","text":", and a fixed point is a self-loop. Also called a successor graph."}],"kind":"symbol","relationshipsSections":[{"identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/DirectedGraph","doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/PropertyGraph","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/s28CustomDebugStringConvertibleP","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/s23CustomStringConvertibleP","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/Se","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/SE","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/SQ","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/s9EscapableP","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/SH","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/s16SendableMetatypeP"],"type":"conformsTo","kind":"relationships","title":"Conforms To"}],"metadata":{"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"FunctionalGraph"}],"remoteSource":{"fileName":"FunctionalGraph.swift","url":"https:\/\/github